   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Limit Values from Graphs",
    "stem": "The graph of f has a left-hand branch approaching an open circle at (2, 3) as x approaches 2 from the left. A right-hand branch approaches an open circle at (2, 5) as x approaches 2 from the right. There is a filled point at (2, 4). What is the two-sided limit of f(x) as x approaches 2?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "3",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Left-side-only trap: 3 is the value approached from the left, but the graph approaches 5 from the right. Considering only one side does not establish a two-sided limit. Fix: Trace both branches toward the input and compare their approached heights."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Right-side-only trap: 5 is the value approached from the right, but the graph approaches 3 from the left. Considering only one side does not establish a two-sided limit. Fix: Check the left-hand approach before concluding from the right-hand branch."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The limit does not exist.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The left-hand limit is 3, while the right-hand limit is 5. A two-sided limit exists only when both one-sided limits agree, so this limit does not exist."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "4",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Function-value trap: The filled point shows f(2) = 4, not the value approached nearby. The two branches approach different heights, neither of which is 4. Fix: Follow the nearby branches rather than using the filled point’s height."
     }
    ]
   },
